    explanation: "Food waste sent to landfill is a hidden climate problem: buried scraps decompose without oxygen and release methane, a greenhouse gas far more potent than CO₂. Composting avoids this by breaking waste down aerobically, and this compost impact calculator shows the difference your compost bin makes. Enter how much food waste you compost each week and it estimates the annual mass you divert from landfill and the greenhouse gas emissions you avoid.\n\nThe logic is straightforward. Your weekly food waste scaled across 52 weeks reveals a yearly total most people underestimate — a household easily composts hundreds of kilograms a year. Applying an emissions factor converts that diverted mass into kilograms of CO₂-equivalent kept out of the atmosphere, representing the methane that landfilling would otherwise have generated.\n\nSeeing both figures makes composting's value tangible beyond the finished soil improver it produces. It reframes food scraps as a resource rather than rubbish and motivates diverting more. Households starting a compost bin, community gardens tracking their impact, and councils promoting food-waste collection all use a compost impact calculator to quantify benefits. Composting also returns nutrients to soil, reducing the need for synthetic fertiliser, so the carbon saving shown here is only part of a wider environmental gain that comes from closing the loop on the food you would otherwise throw away.",
