    formula: "annual kg = bottles/week × 52 × grams ÷ 1000; CO₂ = kg × 6",
    formulaExplanation: "Each plastic bottle weighs a certain number of grams, so your weekly bottle count times its weight gives grams of plastic per week. Multiplying by 52 weeks and dividing by 1,000 converts that to kilograms of plastic per year. Multiplying the annual plastic mass by a factor of about 6 estimates the kilograms of CO₂ emitted producing that plastic, since manufacturing plastic from fossil feedstock releases roughly six times the plastic's own weight in carbon dioxide across its life cycle.",
    explanation: "Single-use plastic bottles are a visible symbol of throwaway culture, and their production carries a real carbon cost on top of the pollution they create. This plastic footprint calculator estimates how much plastic your bottle habit adds up to in a year and the greenhouse gas emissions behind making it. Enter how many plastic bottles you use each week and their typical weight, and it returns your annual plastic consumption in kilograms and the associated CO₂.\n\nThe math is simple but eye-opening. A few bottles a day scales into many kilograms of plastic a year, and because plastic is made from fossil fuels through energy-intensive processes, each kilogram carries roughly six kilograms of CO₂ from its production. The result turns an easily ignored daily habit into a concrete annual figure.\n\nSeeing both numbers motivates change. Switching to a refillable bottle can eliminate almost all of this footprint at a stroke, saving both plastic and carbon while cutting the cost of buying bottled water. People trying to reduce waste, schools teaching sustainability, and offices auditing their consumption use a plastic footprint calculator to quantify single-use plastic and target the easiest wins. While bottles are only one source of plastic, they are among the most avoidable, making this a practical starting point for shrinking your overall plastic and carbon impact over the year.",
